---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import StaticCode from "../../components/StaticCode.astro";

const toc = [
  { id: "overview", label: "Overview" },
  { id: "conventions", label: "Global conventions" },
  { id: "check", label: "check" },
  { id: "diagnostics", label: "Diagnostics" },
  { id: "build", label: "build" },
  { id: "syntax", label: "syntax" },
  { id: "test", label: "test" },
  { id: "prop", label: "prop" },
  { id: "doctor", label: "doctor and demo" },
  { id: "bridge", label: "bridge" },
];

const check = `hs check examples/calculate.hs
hs check local/calculate.hs --profile profiles/my-syntax.json
hs check examples/calculate.hs --json`;
const build = `hs build examples/calculate.hs --target rust --entry calculate

hs build examples/calculate.hs --target csharp --entry calculate \\
  --run --args 10,true`;
const syntax = `hs syntax export examples/calculate.hs \\
  --profile profiles/my-syntax.json \\
  --out local/calculate.hs

hs syntax import local/calculate.hs
hs syntax import local/calculate.hs --dry-run`;
const test = `hs test --all-targets
hs test --quick
hs test --targets csharp,rust --report-dir reports/local --json`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="CLI reference"
  description="Every h# command, flag, output, and exit code, including the editor bridge."
  active="/docs/cli"
  toc={toc}
  prev={{ href: "/docs/profiles", label: "Profiles" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/targets", label: "Targets" }}
>
  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
  <p>
    The command is <code>hs</code>; <code>hsharp</code> and <code>hcharp</code> are equivalent
    aliases. Canonical source uses the standard profile. A personal profile is always selected
    explicitly.
  </p>
  <StaticCode code={`hs check <file> [--profile <profile.json>] [--json]
hs build <file> --target <csharp|rust|go|java|cpp|javascript> [--out <dir>]
          [--entry <function>] [--profile <profile.json>] [--run] [--args a,b]
hs syntax export <canonical.hs> --profile <profile.json> --out <personal.hs>
            [--manifest <file>]
hs syntax import <personal.hs> [--manifest <file>] [--dry-run]
hs test [--all-targets] [--targets <list>] [--quick] [--report-dir <dir>] [--json]
hs prop [--seed <n>] [--count <n>] [--max-size <n>] [--targets <list>]
          [--report-dir <dir>] [--no-reduce] [--quiet] [--json]
hs doctor [--json]
hs demo
hs bridge`} language="bash" title="command summary" />

  <h2 id="conventions">Global conventions</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Aliases</td><td>hs, hsharp, and hcharp run the same CLI.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Help and version</td><td>Use help or --help for usage; use version or --version for the compiler and core versions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Profiles</td><td>Canonical source uses the standard profile. Personal profiles are always selected explicitly.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Streams</td><td>Human diagnostics go to stderr; JSON output goes to stdout.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Output</td><td>Generated projects default to build/program/target.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Exit codes</td><td>0 success, 1 failure, 2 usage error or blocked target.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="check">check</h2>
  <StaticCode code={check} language="bash" title="terminal" />
  <p>
    Parses and checks a file, printing located diagnostics. It does not execute the program and does
    not generate targets. <code>--json</code> emits a machine-readable result with the profile id,
    diagnostics, and function names.
  </p>

  <h2 id="diagnostics">Diagnostics</h2>
  <p>
    Human diagnostics are stable enough to script against. The location comes first, followed by
    severity, diagnostic code, message, and an optional hint on the next line.
  </p>
  <StaticCode code={`examples/calculate.hs:5:12: error E_TYPE_MISMATCH: expected bool, found i32
  hint: use a comparison or an explicit bool expression`} language="text" title="diagnostic format" />
  <p>
    Syntax errors stop after the first parser error. Semantic checks may report several independent
    errors in one pass. JSON output contains the same codes and spans for editor integrations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="build">build</h2>
  <StaticCode code={build} language="bash" title="terminal" />
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--target</code></td><td>Required: <code>csharp</code>, <code>rust</code>, <code>go</code>, <code>java</code>, <code>cpp</code>, or <code>javascript</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--out</code></td><td>Output directory; default <code>build/&lt;program&gt;/&lt;target&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--entry</code></td><td>Entry function. Empty infers <code>main</code> or a single function</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--profile</code></td><td>Interpret the source with this profile</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--run</code></td><td>Compile and execute the generated project</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--args</code></td><td>Comma-separated entry arguments, e.g. <code>10,true</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Generated projects are ordinary: <code>HsProgram.csproj</code> + <code>Program.cs</code>,
    <code>Cargo.toml</code> + <code>src/main.rs</code>, <code>go.mod</code> + <code>main.go</code>,
    <code>HsProgram.java</code>, <code>main.cpp</code>, or <code>main.js</code> +
    <code>package.json</code>. No manual corrections are required.
  </p>

  <h2 id="syntax">syntax</h2>
  <StaticCode code={syntax} language="bash" title="terminal" />
  <p>
    <code>export</code> writes the personal file and a manifest; <code>import</code> validates the
    base and profile, prints a diff, and applies the change. <code>--dry-run</code> stops before
    writing. See <a href="/docs/profiles">Profiles</a> for the manifest schema and conflict rules.
  </p>

  <h2 id="test">test</h2>
  <StaticCode code={test} language="bash" title="terminal" />
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--all-targets</code></td><td>Run every target configured in the manifest</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--targets</code></td><td>Comma-separated target filter</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--quick</code></td><td>Interpreter and structure only; explicitly labelled interpreter-only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--report-dir</code></td><td>Output directory for <code>conformance.json</code> and <code>conformance.md</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--json</code></td><td>Print the complete report JSON</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Exit 0 means every executed check passed. Exit 1 means a failure. Exit 2 means at least one target
    was blocked. Blocked is never counted as a pass.
  </p>

  <h2 id="prop">prop</h2>
  <StaticCode code={`hs prop --seed 1 --count 10 --max-size 3
hs prop --targets csharp,rust --no-reduce --json`} language="bash" title="terminal" />
  <p>
    Generates seeded, type-correct h# programs, runs the reference interpreter and every selected
    target, and compares outcomes including failure codes and source nodes. A divergence is reduced
    by deleting source lines while the failure remains reproducible.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reports are written to reports/property.json and reports/property.md. Exit 0 means every case
    passed, 1 means a failure, and 2 means at least one target was blocked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="doctor">doctor and demo</h2>
  <p>
    <code>hs doctor</code> detects Node, .NET, Cargo, Go, Java, and a C++ compiler, checks
    project-version minimums, prints the versions, and writes <code>reports/environment.json</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>hs demo</code> runs the complete workflow in <code>local/demo/</code>: export to
    <code>my-syntax</code>, edit <code>base + 2</code> to <code>base + 3</code>, reimport, then run
    the reference interpreter and all six targets. It must print <code>33</code> everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bridge">bridge</h2>
  <p>
    <code>hs bridge</code> is a line-delimited JSON service over stdin/stdout. It keeps one process
    alive and caches the profile, which is useful for editors that do not bundle the compiler.
  </p>
  <StaticCode code={`{"id":1,"command":"analyze","text":"fn main() -> i32 { return 1; }","file":"a.hs","targets":["csharp"]}`} language="json" title="request" />
  <StaticCode code={`{"id":1,"ok":true,"command":"analyze","diagnostics":[],"functions":["main"],"entry":"main","timings":{"totalMs":0.4},"generated":[...]}`} language="json" title="response" />
  <p>
    The VS Code extension does not need the bridge: it imports <code>HSharpAnalyzer</code> directly
    into the extension host.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
